# D-148 — THE RECORDS COMING BACK OUT OF A HELPER.
#
# THE GAP: the D-134 scan follows the manifest INTO a helper
# (foundry_validate.py#_fingerprint_inputs indexes a document it never loaded)
# and deliberately does NOT launder taint through a generic loader's path
# argument — a shared `load()` must not poison every caller. The third case is
# neither: a helper handed the ACTUAL manifest, whose RETURN is the records.
#
#   def _rows(m):  return m.get("castings", [])
#   for c in _rows(manifest): c.get("id")
#
# Driven against the shipped `_unestablished_record_indexes` at d8215c5: the
# direct shape was REPORTED and the guarded control was clean, but BOTH the
# one-hop and two-hop shapes came back CLEAN — while both raise on the live
# input the rule names. With `castings` a string, `_rows` returns the string,
# `for c in <str>` iterates characters, and `c.get("id")` is AttributeError.
#
# NO LIVE INSTANCE. All six of the package's real readers are genuinely
# guarded, so this is a derivation gap rather than a live raise. It is filed
# because ST-003's clearance turns on whether the class is UNREPRESENTABLE
# rather than merely absent today: a seventh reader written in this shape would
# not fail on the day it was written.
#
# THE RULE IS A DESCENT TEST, which is what keeps it from becoming the very
# laundering the narrow `_returned_path` was written to avoid. A helper
# propagates its return only when the path it hands back is strictly BELOW what
# it was handed: `_rows` is given the document root and returns `castings`, so
# it descends. A shared `load(path)` returns the same rung it was given, so it
# never propagates however tainted its parameter is.
#
# That guard is now driven on a loader that is GENUINELY seeded. The
# pre-existing plant passed the manifest path as an inline expression, so
# `load` never carried taint on its parameter at all and the laundering guard
# was never really exercised;
# `test_following_a_return_still_does_not_launder_a_shared_loader` BINDS the
# path to a name first, so the loader does carry it, and `reads_state` — which
# reads state.json — still comes back clean.
#
# The call must also be HANDED something tainted before a return resolves, so
# `_rows(anything_else)` is nothing at all; and the two-hop case is reached by
# running the seed and return propagations to a JOINT fixed point, because each
# can feed the other and neither can be computed once and handed to the other.
#
# `seen` is D-142's anchor on the same rows: the guarded control is SEEN and
# cleared, not unseen — the distinction an offender-only scan cannot make, and
# the one it goes blind through.
